00:00:05.724 --> 00:00:08.430
AMY UNRUH: Vertex AI is
an end-to-end platform

00:00:08.430 --> 00:00:10.440
for machine learning
development and deployment.

00:00:10.440 --> 00:00:12.690
It includes a suite of
new products and services

00:00:12.690 --> 00:00:14.485
to support MLOps.

00:00:14.485 --> 00:00:16.110
I'll introduce some
of the key pieces--

00:00:16.110 --> 00:00:19.380
Vertex pipelines, feature
store, and model monitoring.

00:00:19.380 --> 00:00:21.375
The backbone of the
Vertex MLOps story

00:00:21.375 --> 00:00:24.480
is Vertex pipelines, which lets
you automate and orchestrate

00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:26.100
your machine learning workflows.

00:00:26.100 --> 00:00:29.760
Vertex pipelines is scalable,
serverless, and cost-effective,

00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:33.602
and supports GCP standard
enterprise security controls.

00:00:33.602 --> 00:00:36.060
You can focus on specifying
your pipelines without worrying

00:00:36.060 --> 00:00:38.700
about infrastructure, and you
pay only for the resources

00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:40.800
that you use.

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:42.960
When pipelines
execute, run metadata

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:44.580
is automatically logged.

00:00:44.580 --> 00:00:47.370
This allows linage
tracking, reproducibility,

00:00:47.370 --> 00:00:52.120
comparisons across runs,
metrics visualization, and more.

00:00:52.120 --> 00:00:55.500
Let's take a quick look at how
these pipelines are defined.

00:00:55.500 --> 00:00:59.520
Vertex pipeline supports two
easy-to-use Python STKs--

00:00:59.520 --> 00:01:01.770
KFP and TFX.

00:01:01.770 --> 00:01:04.620
Pipelines are built using
reusable components,

00:01:04.620 --> 00:01:07.320
and many pre-built
components exist, including

00:01:07.320 --> 00:01:10.050
those for Vertex AI services.

00:01:10.050 --> 00:01:14.670
Here, we're building an auto ML
image classification workflow.

00:01:14.670 --> 00:01:17.190
We'll just compile this
pipeline and run it right

00:01:17.190 --> 00:01:19.900
from the notebook like this.

00:01:19.900 --> 00:01:23.370
Then, we can view the running
pipeline in the console UI.

00:01:23.370 --> 00:01:26.470
While the pipeline is running
or after it is finished,

00:01:26.470 --> 00:01:28.830
you can view the pipeline
step logs and generated

00:01:28.830 --> 00:01:30.825
artifacts and metadata.

00:01:30.825 --> 00:01:32.700
And if you want to build
your own components,

00:01:32.700 --> 00:01:34.710
this is easy to do as well.

00:01:34.710 --> 00:01:37.740
Automating orchestration is not
the complete picture, though.

00:01:37.740 --> 00:01:39.600
One common set of
pain points revolves

00:01:39.600 --> 00:01:43.290
around feature engineering,
feature reuse and sharing,

00:01:43.290 --> 00:01:46.110
serving features at scale
and with low latency,

00:01:46.110 --> 00:01:49.000
and avoiding training
serving skew.

00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:51.760
The Vertex feature store
helps solve these problems.

00:01:51.760 --> 00:01:54.220
It reduces the cycle
time of ML deployments

00:01:54.220 --> 00:01:57.280
by making it easy to share,
discover, and serve features

00:01:57.280 --> 00:01:58.370
at scale.

00:01:58.370 --> 00:02:01.360
After you've trained, evaluated,
and deployed your model,

00:02:01.360 --> 00:02:02.247
you're not done.

00:02:02.247 --> 00:02:04.330
You still need to pay
attention to the performance

00:02:04.330 --> 00:02:05.890
of your model over time.

00:02:05.890 --> 00:02:08.800
If model drift occurs, it
may be necessary to retrain

00:02:08.800 --> 00:02:10.600
or tune the model.

00:02:10.600 --> 00:02:12.970
Vertex model monitoring
helps with both alerting

00:02:12.970 --> 00:02:14.380
and diagnosis.

00:02:14.380 --> 00:02:17.020
You can set up a monitoring
job for a deployed model,

00:02:17.020 --> 00:02:19.390
and get alerts when
there are anomalies.

00:02:19.390 --> 00:02:22.300
You can configure monitoring
objectives for model features

00:02:22.300 --> 00:02:24.250
and get distribution stats.

00:02:24.250 --> 00:02:27.300
[MUSIC PLAYING]

